        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>Since the late 1960s, the immune system has been studied by United States and Russian space scientists.  Depressed immune cell function has been confirmed in-flight by reduced responses to in vivo delayed-type hypersensitivity tests.  Changes in cell-mediated immune function have been consistently documented.  However, the effect of space flight on the humoral immune system has not been thoroughly assessed during flight.  Therefore, the goal of this experiment was to test whether space flight has an adverse affect on humoral immune function similar to that observed with the cell-mediated immune system.  It was expected, based on the decrease in other immune system functions, that the ability to mount an antibody response to new or recall antigens would be depressed during space flight.  It was hypothesized that the specific antibody titers following immunization during flight would be significantly lower than the titers obtained from a ground-based control group.  The objectives of this experiment were to (1) determine the effects of long duration space flight on baseline levels of immunoglobulins (IgG, IgA, IgD, IgE and IgM) in serum (2) assess the ability to produce appropriate antibodies in response to a specific antigenic challenge during space flight and (3) determine secretory immune function by measuring salivary IgA and lysozyme levels in response to immunization during space flight.<P>APPROACH:<br>An in-flight vaccination with specific antigens (23 pneumococcal polysaccharide antigens) was used to test the ability to mount an antibody response <I>in vivo</I>.  These antigens produce well-characterized antibody responses in normal, healthy individuals.  Blood and saliva samples were collected prior to flight and immediately before in-flight immunization to determine the baseline values of the specific pneumococcal antibodies.  Vaccination took place during the late flight period.  Additional saliva and serum samples were scheduled to be taken 7, 14 and 21 days after the in-flight immunization to measure the magnitude of antibody production.  It should be noted that in-flight immunization was performed on two out of three subjects scheduled to receive antigen injections.  In addition, the post flight sample collection schedule was significantly modified due to crewmember and medical requirements.  The final post flight sample collection was delayed for two crewmembers.  The result was that samples were not collected on immunization+21 days (3 weeks after immunization).  Samples were collected at 4 weeks after immunization for the two crewmembers, therefore, the peak antibody response was missed.   Antibodies to the immunization antigens were measured by Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA) in the serum and saliva samples.  The degree of response was compared to those obtained from a ground-based control group which were age- and sex-matched volunteer subjects.<P>RESULTS:<br>The three crewmembers all exhibited minor shifts in the levels of isotype specific antibodies between the preflight and in-flight pre-immunization period.  In general, these variations did not appear to be highly significant, and they suggest the serum antibody levels were not altered directly by space flight.  The immunized crewmembers did respond to the vaccine, indicated by the increase of antigen-specific antibody titers.  However, the magnitude of response may be altered for some isotypes in some individuals.  The degree of the response was not particularly robust in one crewmember.  It was found that antibody titers of two of the four isotypes doubled the pre-immunization levels but did not reach 200 nanograms antibody-nitrogen/ml for one crewmember.  Incidentally, a positive response is a two-fold increase over the baseline level and a post-immunization titer above 300 nanograms antibody-nitrogen/ml.  However, since the data set is very small, this data may suggest a decreased response to the vaccine in this crewmember, or it could be a function of individual variability since some of the isotype levels were adequate.   General immunoglobulin levels did not change significantly during or after flight in any of the crewmembers.<END>
        </div>

